<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><%- include('../partials/head') %></head>
<body>
<div class="app">
<%- include('../partials/sidebar') %>
<%- include('../partials/topbar') %>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="hint mt-0">
        <strong>Two kinds of demand, discovered separately.</strong>
        Search-box queries come from Search Console — real impressions for this exact site, not an estimate —
        extended with Google's own autocomplete, an alphabet sweep of it, and the engine's own related-search
        suggestions. Assistant prompts are written by the AI model, because the sentences people type into
        ChatGPT are whole questions with a stated situation and cannot be derived from a keyword list.
      </p>
      <p class="hint">
        <strong>Volume and difficulty, with the source named on every value.</strong>
        Volume is taken from the best source configured — Google Ads Keyword Planner, DataForSEO or Semrush —
        and where none is configured the two honest alternatives are shown in their own columns instead: Search
        Console impressions (a measurement of this site) and Google Trends relative interest for the country you
        pick (0-100, the shape of demand rather than its size). Difficulty is the vendor's where a credential
        provides one, otherwise computed from a keyless sample of a non-Google result page with the formula and
        every component shown. Nothing is ever invented, and nothing labelled "measured" is not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%- include('_partials/brandpicker') %>

  <% if (brand) { %>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-head"><h3>Run research</h3></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <form method="POST" action="/ai-seo/research/run">
        <input type="hidden" name="brand_id" value="<%= brand.id %>">
        <div class="field">
          <label for="seeds">Seed topics <span class="muted">(one per line — leave empty to use this site's own top non-branded queries)</span></label>
          <textarea id="seeds" name="seeds" rows="4" placeholder="compliance certification&#10;aml training&#10;basel iii course"><%= brand.seed_topics || '' %></textarea>
        </div>
        <div class="field-row">
          <label class="field">
            <span>Search Console window</span>
            <select name="days">
              <option value="28">28 days</option>
              <option value="90" selected>90 days</option>
              <option value="180">180 days</option>
              <option value="365">365 days</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="field">
            <span>Country <span class="muted">(volume, difficulty and autocomplete)</span></span>
            <select name="country">
              <% marketList.forEach(function (m) { %>
                <option value="<%= m.code %>" <%= m.code === selectedMarket ? 'selected' : '' %>><%= m.name %></option>
              <% }) %>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label class="field">
            <span>Difficulty for the top</span>
            <select name="difficulty_limit">
              <option value="0">none — skip difficulty</option>
              <option value="6">6 keywords — fastest</option>
              <option value="12" selected>12 keywords</option>
              <option value="25">25 keywords</option>
              <option value="40">40 keywords — slow</option>
            </select>
          </label>
        </div>
        <p class="hint mt-0">
          The country reaches every geo-aware source in the identifier it expects: Google's <code>gl</code>/<code>hl</code>
          for autocomplete, a <code>geo</code> code for Trends, a region for the result sample, and a location
          code for any paid adapter. Passing the wrong one silently returns worldwide data labelled as a
          country, which is the worst failure available here.
        </p>
        <p class="hint mt-0">
          Difficulty is capped because, with no vendor credential, each keyword costs one paced request to a
          result page. Whatever the cap leaves out is reported rather than left blank-looking — a keyword with no
          difficulty shows an em dash, never a zero.
        </p>
        <div class="checkbox-row">
          <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="include_suggest" checked> Expand seeds through Google autocomplete <span class="muted">(question, comparison, commercial and local modifiers)</span></label>
        </div>
        <div class="checkbox-row">
          <label class="check">
            <input type="checkbox" name="alphabet_sweep" checked>
            Also sweep the alphabet
            <span class="muted">— "&lt;seed&gt; a", "&lt;seed&gt; b" … Google completes each prefix differently, and this is where the long-tail phrasings a modifier list cannot anticipate come from. It is the technique behind every keyword tool's long tail, and it is free.</span>
          </label>
        </div>
        <div class="checkbox-row">
          <label class="check">
            <input type="checkbox" name="include_related" checked>
            Add related searches
            <span class="muted">— autocomplete completes what you typed; related searches are the engine's own list of other ways to ask the same thing, which is where a topic's synonyms and adjacent framings come from. Autocomplete alone was missing half of "keyword suggestions based on Google data".</span>
          </label>
        </div>
        <div class="checkbox-row">
          <label class="check">
            <input type="checkbox" name="include_metrics" checked>
            Look up search volume and keyword difficulty
            <span class="muted">— tries Keyword Planner, DataForSEO and Semrush in order, then falls back to Google Trends relative interest and a SERP-based difficulty proxy. Every rung tried is reported on the result page, including the ones that were not configured.</span>
          </label>
        </div>
        <div class="checkbox-row">
          <label class="check">
            <input type="checkbox" name="include_prompts" <%= aiAvailable ? 'checked' : 'disabled' %>>
            Write the AI-assistant prompts for these topics
            <% if (!aiAvailable) { %><span class="muted">— needs an Azure OpenAI credential</span><% } %>
          </label>
        </div>
        <div class="checkbox-row">
          <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="force" value="1"> Regenerate the AI half rather than reusing the cached answer</label>
        </div>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <button type="submit" class="btn" <%= perms.canWrite ? '' : 'disabled' %>>Run research</button>
          <%
          /* Same textarea, different endpoint. `formaction` on a second submit
             button means the seeds are saved from the field the user just
             typed into, rather than from a hidden copy that would go stale the
             moment they edited it. `name="seed_topics"` on the textarea is
             what /seeds reads. */
          %>
          <button type="submit" class="btn secondary" formaction="/ai-seo/research/seeds"
                  <%= perms.canWrite ? '' : 'disabled' %>
                  title="Store these seeds on the brand so the next run and the scheduled sweep reuse them">
            Save seeds to brand
          </button>
        </div>
        <% if (!brand.gsc_property) { %>
          <p class="hint">
            This brand has no Search Console property paired, so the measured half will be empty and everything
            will rest on autocomplete and the model. <a href="/brands/<%= brand.id %>">Pair a property</a> to fix that.
          </p>
        <% } %>
        <input type="hidden" name="seed_topics" id="seed_topics_mirror" value="<%= brand.seed_topics || '' %>">
      </form>
      <script>
      // "Save seeds to brand" posts to a different endpoint, which reads
      // `seed_topics`. Mirroring the textarea into a hidden field on submit
      // keeps one visible control instead of two that can disagree.
      (function () {
        var ta = document.getElementById('seeds');
        var mirror = document.getElementById('seed_topics_mirror');
        if (!ta || !mirror) return;
        var sync = function () { mirror.value = ta.value; };
        ta.addEventListener('input', sync);
        if (ta.form) ta.form.addEventListener('submit', sync);
        sync();
      }());
      </script>
    </div>
  </div>
  <% } %>

  <%- include('_partials/runs') %>

<%- include('../partials/sidebar-end') %>
<%- include('../partials/foot') %>
